                    new[] { "Because it forces the garbage collector to free memory immediately.", "Because structs cannot have methods unless they are mutating.", "Because it notifies the compiler to execute the method on a background thread.", "Because value types are immutable by default within their own instance methods; `mutating` allows modifying properties." },
                    3, "Structs are value types; modifying their properties changes the value itself, requiring explicit permission via the `mutating` keyword. Assuming structs behave like reference types causes compile errors. This enforces deliberate state modification.",
                    skillId: 10),
                CreateQuestion(id++, "How does Automatic Reference Counting (ARC) differ from a traditional Garbage Collector?",
                    QuestionCategory.Technical, JobTitleRoleFamily.Mobile,
                    QuestionDifficulty.Medium, ExperienceSeniorityLevel.Mid,
                    new[] { "There is no difference; ARC is just Apple's marketing name for a standard Garbage Collector.", "ARC tracks reference counts deterministically at compile-time and deallocates immediately at runtime when count hits zero, whereas a GC runs a periodic background trace process.", "ARC requires developers to manually call `retain()` and `release()`.", "ARC only manages memory for structs, while GC manages memory for classes." },
                    1, "ARC inserts memory management instructions (`retain`/`release`) at compile time, causing instant, deterministic deallocation when an object's reference count hits zero. Assuming it runs a background trace process like Java's GC ignores ARC's performance predictability. It prevents background CPU spikes common in GC languages.",
                    skillId: 10),
                CreateQuestion(id++, "What is the purpose of a `lazy` stored property in Swift?",
