                    QuestionDifficulty.Hard, ExperienceSeniorityLevel.Senior,
                    new[] { "`weak` references become `nil` when the object is deallocated and must be optionals. `unowned` references assume the object will never be `nil` and will crash if accessed after deallocation.", "`weak` is for classes while `unowned` is for structs.", "`unowned` retains the object in memory longer than `weak`.", "They are completely identical; `unowned` is just a legacy Objective-C term." },
                    0, "Both break retain cycles, but `unowned` crashes if accessed after deallocation, acting like a forced-unwrapped optional, while `weak` safely becomes `nil`. Using `unowned` carelessly when lifetimes aren't guaranteed leads to fatal crashes. Choosing correctly demonstrates expert memory management.",
                    skillId: 10),
                CreateQuestion(id++, "How does Protocol-Oriented Programming (POP) reduce code duplication in Swift?",
