                    new[] { "By replacing all class inheritance with massive global functions.", "By restricting methods to only take generic `Any` parameters.", "By using protocol extensions to provide default implementations for methods, allowing structs and classes to share logic without inheritance.", "By compiling code down to C-level macros automatically." },
                    2, "Protocol extensions allow developers to inject default method implementations directly into protocols. Assuming POP relies on global functions misses its elegance. This paradigm replaces bloated base classes with highly composable, reusable behaviors.",
                    skillId: 10),
                CreateQuestion(id++, "How does Swift determine method dispatch at runtime compared to Objective-C?",
                    QuestionCategory.Technical, JobTitleRoleFamily.Mobile,
                    QuestionDifficulty.Hard, ExperienceSeniorityLevel.Senior,
                    new[] { "Swift prefers static (direct) dispatch for performance, falling back to dynamic dispatch for class inheritance, whereas Objective-C relies entirely on dynamic message sending.", "Swift always uses dynamic message sending for every function call, making it identical to Objective-C.", "Swift offloads all method dispatching to the LLVM compiler, bypassing runtime execution.", "Swift only supports static dispatch, making polymorphism impossible." },
                    0, "Swift aggressively optimizes using static (direct) dispatch for speed, only resorting to slower dynamic dispatch (via vtables) for classes, and message dispatch for `@objc`. Assuming it acts exactly like Objective-C ignores Swift's extreme performance gains. Understanding dispatch helps optimize CPU-intensive code.",
                    skillId: 10),
                CreateQuestion(id++, "What is a Property Wrapper (`@propertyWrapper`) in Swift?",
